<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Abbey of Corvey</h1><p class="st-meta">822 CE – 1803 CE · capital Höxter</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Carolingian abbey of Saxony, a base for missions to the north and home of the chronicler Widukind, whose westwork is a World Heritage Site.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Corvey was founded in 822 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoxter">Höxter</a> by monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corbie-abbey">Corbie</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalard-of-corbie">Adalard</a> and Wala, cousins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. It was intended to anchor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in newly conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and in 836 it received the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vitus">Saint Vitus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis">Saint-Denis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Corvey sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> on his mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and its school produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>, whose Deeds of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a> is a chief source for the Ottonian kings. The abbots were princes of the Empire ruling a small territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoxter">Höxter</a>. Its Carolingian westwork, completed in 885, is one of the few surviving from the period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Corvey shrank after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War. In 1792 it was raised to a prince-bishopric, but in 1803 it was secularised and given to the Prince of Orange-Nassau-Fulda, then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-von-fallersleben">Hoffmann von Fallersleben</a>, author of the German national anthem, later worked in its library.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-abbey-of-corvey.jpg" alt="Prince-Abbey of Corvey" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Aeggy · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corvey_Westwerk_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>822 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Höxter</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adalard, Wala, Widukind of Corvey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Saxon, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Corvey, Prince-Bishopric of Corvey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1312&amp;focus=prince-abbey-of-corvey" aria-label="Prince-Abbey of Corvey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4892 387 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Abbey of Corvey lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5212 643L5229 694M5212 643L5183 683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5183 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 643h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 643h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1312&amp;focus=prince-abbey-of-corvey">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
